# Architecture

`__APP_TITLE__` is __APP_ARCHITECTURE__. The app owns its domain model, data access, and all content below `AppShell`. The platform owns the shell, shared interaction primitives, structural styles, and live cosmetic theme.

Import shared components only from `@local-web/ui` and the Vite integration only from `@local-web/ui/vite`. Keep domain-specific components inside this repository. Add libraries only when the domain needs them; the platform does not require a router, state library, or backend framework.

The checked-in `vendor/local-web-ui.tgz` is the immutable UI package used by this app. Its expected platform version is `__UI_VERSION__`. Update it through the central platform tooling rather than editing the artifact.

`src/contextExport.ts` is app-owned and supplies the shared shell's Context export action. It starts with only the generated foundation state; extend its structured context as domain features gain reviewed, shareable current-state information.

## Optional interactive HTML export

UI 0.7.0 adds a separate self-contained interactive snapshot; template version 3
and the existing script-free `LocalWebContextV1` context export are unchanged.
Apps that do not opt in retain their shell markup and one-click `Export context`.
Interactive-only apps show `Export interactive snapshot`; both capabilities use
an accessible `Export` dialog offering `Context document` and `Interactive snapshot`.

The app owns inclusion decisions, a versioned payload contract, coherent capture,
one shared decoder, an offline entry reusing app components/content, controlled
restorable state, explicit backend-action adaptations and domain acceptance tests.
The framework owns the common envelope, canonical plain-JSON safety, identity
checks, Vite packaging, download lifecycle, artifact CSP, offline notice and theme.
It does not scrape the DOM, redact private data, replay mutations or synthesize APIs.

`snapshotData` is frozen domain content needed by the reader (records, results,
transformed service responses). `viewState` is the presentation position (tab,
selection, filters, sort order). They are independent app-defined plain-JSON
shapes: `{}` is valid for compiled/static snapshot content and for empty view state.
Functions, dates, class instances, accessors, cycles, sparse arrays, non-finite
numbers and unsafe object keys are rejected.

Integration is app-owned, after adopting the UI through the central updater's
reviewed dry-run/apply path, never by editing `vendor/local-web-ui.tgz`:

1. Define `src/contract.ts` with `defineInteractiveExportContract({ id, version,
   sensitivity, decodeSnapshot })` imported from `@local-web/ui`; export it as
   named `contract` or default. Use a canonical lowercase ID, positive integer
   version and a non-empty explicit notice for either `private` or `sensitive`.
   Increment the version for incompatible payload shape or semantic changes.
2. Implement the shared app-owned decoder to accept unknown input and return a
   typed `{ snapshotData, viewState, title? }` or throw. Validate both shapes and
   relationships, such as selected IDs existing in records or compiled content.
   Import that exact module in hosted and offline entries. Decoder details stay
   app-local; the framework shows generic errors.
3. Supply `{ contract, buildSnapshot }` to `<AppShell app={app}
   interactiveExport={interactiveExport}>…</AppShell>`. The builder closes over
   current state, honours `AbortSignal`, and returns one coherent capture.
4. Add `localWebInteractiveExport` alongside `localWebApp()` and existing Vite
   plugins, importing build APIs only from `@local-web/ui/vite`:

   ```ts
   localWebInteractiveExport({
     appId: '__APP_ID__', contract: './src/contract.ts', entry: './src/offline.tsx',
   });
   ```

   These are module paths, not a contract metadata object. Build identity comes
   from `local-web.json`, `package.json` and Git `HEAD`. Apps supply React/React DOM
   peers, their real TypeScript declarations, and Vite when using the build API.
5. In `src/offline.tsx`, import the same contract, app components and styles, then
   call `mountInteractiveExport({ contract, render: ({ snapshotData, viewState }) =>
   <App snapshotData={snapshotData} initialViewState={viewState} /> })` from
   `@local-web/ui`. Lift UI state as needed instead of copying the app for export.

For services, prefer one coherent same-origin snapshot of related state over
racing requests. The framework invokes the decoder after `buildSnapshot()` and
before fetching a template or downloading, then again before offline rendering.
The first call validates the actual backend response: an unchanged template does
not prove a changed backend response satisfies the contract.

`payloadContractId` is `<app-id>/<contract-id>/v<version>`, the semantic agreement.
`templateId` is SHA-256 over the normalized self-contained reader including code,
styles/assets, shell, CSP, schema and app/build/contract metadata, excluding capture
data, source maps and build paths. `compatibilityId` hashes schema, app ID, payload
contract ID and template ID together. Hosted descriptor, fetched template and
envelope must agree on all three before the app decoder proves payload semantics.
The plugin emits a content-addressed same-origin template and automatically embeds
the shell descriptor; the typed `virtual:local-web-interactive-export` module is
also available. A stale open page fetches only its exact template and fails closed
if missing or incompatible: no `latest` fallback or cross-version mixing.

Notices appear before capture and in the artifact; classification is not encryption
or redaction. Include only deliberate content, never credentials, environment
values or unrelated private data. Snapshot data has a 5 MiB canonical-JSON limit
covering `snapshotData`, `viewState` and optional title together
(`MAX_INTERACTIVE_EXPORT_SNAPSHOT_BYTES`); complete HTML has a separate 20 MiB
limit (`MAX_INTERACTIVE_EXPORT_ARTIFACT_BYTES`), neither app-overridable. Failures
are generic and start no partial download; abort is normal, duplicate generation
is blocked, focus is restored and anchors/object URLs are cleaned up.

The single HTML file contains a classic runtime, styles, supported visual assets
and an encoded canonical envelope in a non-executable `<template>`. There are no
automatic network dependencies, external modules, source maps, sibling assets,
CDNs or hosted services. Deliberate evidence hyperlinks remain allowed. The exact
artifact policy is separate from hosted CSP and unchanged context-document CSP:

```text
default-src 'none'; connect-src 'none'; script-src 'sha256-<packaged-script-digest>'; script-src-attr 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data: blob:; font-src data:; media-src data: blob:; worker-src blob:; child-src blob:; object-src 'none'; frame-src 'none'; manifest-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
```

Only the hashed packaged script can execute; no inline event handlers or
`unsafe-eval`. `connect-src 'none'` blocks fetch, XHR, WebSocket, EventSource and
beacons. Inline styles allow React attributes while other directives block remote
resources. Browsers ignore `frame-ancestors` in meta CSP, so this is not an
anti-framing guarantee.

Offline shell context is inert `Offline snapshot`, with no System Index link or
recursive exports. The notice shows classification, explicit notice, capture time,
source revision and frozen-content status. Bundled fallback theme/styles replace
the hosted theme endpoint; captured effective light/dark mode is restored.
System/Light/Dark controls use memory, never host storage; reload restores the
capture. Use `useInteractiveExportEnvironment()` to freeze backend data read-only,
disable actions with an offline explanation, replace them with wholly local
interactions, or keep deliberate external links. Never silently reconnect or replay
backend mutations.

App acceptance must cover decoder rejection/relationship cases, the real browser
download opened at `file://` with networking disabled, all exportable interactions,
keyboard/focus, accessibility, theme restoration, and desktop/390px/320px layouts
in light/dark modes. Observe automatic network attempts as well as CSP enforcement.
Doctor remains structural; it does not validate payload semantics. UI adoption,
source integration, activation, deployment and push remain separate boundaries.
